{{wp}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*meh₂d-|id=meet}}
From {{inh|en|enm|mōt}}, {{m|enm|ȝemōt}}, from {{inh|en|ang|*mōt}}, {{m|ang|ġemōt||meeting}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*mōtą}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*meh₂d-|id=meet|t=to encounter, come}}. Cognate with {{cog|sco|mut}}, {{m|sco|mote||meeting, assembly}}, {{cog|nds|Mööt||meeting}}, {{m|nds|Moot||meeting}}, archaic Dutch {{m|nl|moet|(ge)moet|meeting}}, {{cog|da|møde||meeting}}, {{cog|sv|möte||meeting}}, {{cog|no|møte||meeting}}, {{cog|is|mót||meeting, tournament, meet}}. Related to {{m|en|meet}}. The adjective derives from the noun.

====Pronunciation====
* {{enPR|mo͞ot}}, {{IPA|en|/muːt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-moot.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-moot.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|uːt|s=1}}

====Adjective====
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|current in|_|UK|rare|_|in the|_|US}} Subject to discussion (originally at a [[#Noun|moot]]); [[arguable]], [[debatable]], [[unsolved]] or impossible to solve.
#* '''1770''', {{w|Joseph Banks}}, ''The [[w:HMS Bark Endeavour|Endeavour]] Journal of Sir Joseph Banks'', [[s:The Endeavour Journal of Sir Joseph Banks/January 1770|January 4, 1770]] (published 1962):
#*: {{...}}:indeed we were obligd to hawl off rather in a hurry for the wind freshning a little we found ourselves in a bay which it was a '''moot''' point whether or not we could get out of:{{...}}
#* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|[[s:Moby-Dick (1851) US edition/Chapter 32|Chapter 32]]|text=[T]he uncertain, unsettled condition of this science of Cetology is in the very vestibule attested by the fact, that in some quarters it still remains a '''moot''' point whether a whale be a fish.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|author={{w|Colin Jones (historian)|Colin Jones}}|title=The Great Nation|page=477|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2003
|passage=The extent to which these Parisian radicals ‘represented’ the French people as a whole was very '''moot'''.}}
# {{lb|en|North America|chiefly|legal}} Being an exercise of [[thought]]; [[academic]].
# {{senseid|en|irrelevant}}{{lb|en|North America|uncommon|_|in the|_|UK}} Having no practical [[consequence]] or [[relevance]].
#: {{syn|en|irrelevant|obsolete<q:if it was previously relevant>}}
#: {{ux|en|That point may make for a good discussion, but it is '''moot'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2007|author=Paul Mankowski|title=The Languages of Biblical Translation|titleurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20080531094333/http://www.adoremus.org/0607Translations.html|journal=Adoremus Bulletin|volume=13|issue=4
|passage=The question [whether certain poetry was present in the original Hebrew Psalms] in our own time is '''moot''', since various considerations have made it certain that, of all the hazards presented by biblical translation, a dangerous excess of beauty is not one of them.}}

