===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|moten|t=to speak, talk, converse, discuss|id=to speak}}, from {{inh|en|ang|mōtian||to speak, converse, discuss}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*mōtijaną||to meet, encounter}}, a suffixed derivative of {{m|gem-pro|*mōtą||meeting}}. Related to etymology 1. See also {{m|en|mutter}} (which is a [[frequentative]] of ''moot'').

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{senseid|en|bring up for debate}} To bring up as a subject for [[debate#Noun|debate]].
#: {{syn|en|table<id:add to the agenda>|propose#Verb}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|author=Howard Davies|title=Will the UK really turn into 'Singapore-on-Thames' after Brexit?|date=2019-12-17|url=https://www.theguardian.com/business/2019/dec/17/uk-singapore-on-thames-brexit-france|passage=The general idea was first '''mooted''' a couple of years ago by Philip Hammond, then Britain’s chancellor of the exchequer, as a means of encouraging the EU to strike a friendly Brexit deal with the UK.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261013061549/https://www.theguardian.com/business/2019/dec/17/uk-singapore-on-thames-brexit-france|archivedate=13 October 2026}}
# To [[discuss]] or [[debate#Verb|debate]].
#: {{syn|en|bespeak|talk about|treat|Thesaurus:discuss}}
#* {{RQ:Hamilton Metaphysics and Logic
|passage=a problem which hardly has been mentioned, far less '''mooted''', in this country}}
#* {{RQ:Elyot Governour
|passage=First a case is appointed to be '''mooted''' by certain young men, containing some doubtful controversy.}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|date=2015-03-04|author=Peter Shadbolt| title=Amazing Cycle Super Highways| url=http://www.cnn.com/2015/03/04/tech/city-cycle-super-highways/index.html| accessdate=2015-03-11| passage=An elevated cycleway connecting Los Angeles and Pasadena was '''mooted''' as early as 1896 …|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230610045930/https://www.cnn.com/2015/03/04/tech/city-cycle-super-highways/index.html|archivedate=10 June 2023}}
# {{senseid|en|make irrelevant}} {{lb|en|US}} To make or declare [[irrelevant]].
# To argue or plead in a supposed case.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1641|author=w:Ben Jonson|title=Timber
|passage=There is a difference between '''mooting''' and pleading; between fencing and fighting.}}
# {{lb|en|regional|obsolete}} To [[talk]] or [[speak]].
#: {{syn|en|jabber|say|Thesaurus:speak}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1535|author={{w|William Stewart (bishop of Aberdeen)|William Stewart}}|title=The Buik of the Croniclis of Scotland|url=https://books.google.ru/books?id=EH0PUB-Q7dgC&printsec=frontcover&dq=The+Buik+of+the+Croniclis+of+Scotland&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjJ0dG5o9PWAhVMBsAKHR9VBKAQ6AEIMDAB#v=onepage&q&f=false
|passage=In that mater now I will '''mute '''no moir.}}
# {{lb|en|Scotland|Northern England}} To [[say]], [[utter]], also [[insinuate]].
#: {{syn|en|hint|indicate|Thesaurus:allude}}

=====Usage notes=====
* In the fifth sense, usually found in the archaic phrase '''no boot to moot''', as in: ''it's no boot to moot with her'' (it is no use to talk/reason/plead with her).

* In rural northern dialects of the UK, usually used together with the verbs {{m|en|mell}} and {{m|en|spell}}, where {{m|en|moot}} is used instead of {{m|en|talk}} and {{m|en|say}}; {{m|en|mell}} used instead of {{m|en|speak}} and {{m|en|converse}}; and {{m|en|spell}} instead of {{m|en|tell}} and {{m|en|relate}}. The verb {{m|en|moot}} in the sense ''to talk, say, utter etc.'', is part of an informal in-group speak or register wherein speakers (mostly of northern dialects) use this and the above-mentioned words when talking with one another and when talking with outsiders or strangers they, usually, only use the words like ''say'', ''talk'', ''speak'' etc. For example, if a mother is talking with her child she is much more likely to use words like ''moot'', ''mell'' and ''spell'', however if she is speaking with a stranger from the South she is extremely unlikely to use such words. Also, such words are usually considered taboo in formal contexts.

